\section{Molecular similarity and activity cliffs}\label{sec:mol_similarity}

% TODO: review, needs manual review
Much of cheminformatics rests on the similar property principle, which states that structurally similar molecules tend to have similar properties \citep{Johnson1990, vanTilborg2022}.
One of its clearest applications is nearest-neighbor prediction, which appears across cheminformatics workflows and, in this thesis, in the kernels used by GPs and at the basis of many noise identification algorithms.

% TODO: review, needs manual review
The difficulty with this principle is that similarity is defined only relative to a similarity measure, which is fixed by the choice of representation and
coefficient \citep{Maggiora2014}. There is no standard definition of similarity between molecules, especially when different molecular embeddings are handled in a computational setting. Two molecules that are near-identical under a substructure fingerprint may be far apart under a PDV, and \citet{Maggiora2014} caution that quantified similarity can be over-interpreted in this way.

% CODE INPUT: formula added so that Chapter 4's argument about distances has its starting point here
The most common measure for binary fingerprints is the Tanimoto coefficient \citep{Bajusz2015}. For two molecules with fingerprints $\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{x}' \in \{0,1\}^d$,
\begin{equation}
T(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{x}') = \frac{\sum_{j=1}^{d} x_j x'_j}{\sum_{j=1}^{d} x_j + \sum_{j=1}^{d} x'_j - \sum_{j=1}^{d} x_j x'_j},
\label{eq:tanimoto}
\end{equation}
which is the number of bits set in both molecules divided by the number of bits set in either. Continuous representations are instead compared with a distance, most often the Euclidean distance $\sqrt{\sum_{j=1}^{d}(x_j - x'_j)^2}$. Both measures are sums over the features, and neither weighs a feature by its relation to the property. In the Tanimoto coefficient, a bit that is off in both molecules contributes nothing, whereas every bit that is set in either molecule counts. In the Euclidean distance, every feature that differs between the two molecules counts. Chapter~\ref{chap:flaws} returns to this point (Equation~\ref{eq:distance_split}).

% [CLAUDE 2026-10-09] citation fix: Bajusz2015 does not compare strong and weak representations; that part is now marked as our inference.
Distance-based reasoning relies on the assumption that the data embeddings contain quality information. When the labels are corrupted or the important features are scarce, similarity scores over-rely on compounding false or useless information, and the assumption that similar molecules have similar properties breaks down. \citet{Bajusz2015} found that the Tanimoto, Dice and Cosine coefficients and the Soergel distance rank molecules almost identically on binary fingerprints. We take this to mean that changing the formula cannot compensate for a weak representation.
This problem is further complicated by activity cliffs (defined below). These molecules, which potentially contain the most important information for model prediction, can be confused with noise. Another barrier to distance-based reasoning is persistent noise across labels. The task of trying to determine if a molecule's value matches that of its neighbors becomes a lot harder when all of the neighbors have some form of noise as well.

There are many downstream tasks which rely on the concept of similarity. The second half of this thesis relies on many such tasks, and as such focuses on identifying representations that agree with this principle. 


% TODO: review, needs manual review
The sharpest failure of the similar property principle is the activity cliff, a pair of structurally similar molecules with a large difference in potency that is produced by a small
chemical change \citep{Maggiora2006, Stumpfe2012}. Activity cliffs inherit the measure dependence of the principle they violate, and there is no widely accepted definition of one
\citep{vanTilborg2022}. The MoleculeACE benchmark \citep{vanTilborg2022} makes this choice explicit. A pair is considered highly similar if its similarity exceeds 90\% under at least one of
three measures, namely the Tanimoto coefficient on ECFPs, the Tanimoto coefficient on scaffold fingerprints and a scaled Levenshtein distance on SMILES strings. Such a pair forms a cliff if
the two molecules also differ tenfold or more in $K_i$ or EC$_{50}$ \citep{vanTilborg2022}. This soft consensus over three measures is used because each measure captures only part of the
underlying chemistry. Across the thirty MoleculeACE targets, cliff compounds account for between 7\% and 52\% of a dataset \citep{vanTilborg2022}.

% TODO: review, needs manual review
% [CLAUDE 2026-10-09] citation fix: the convergence of nearest and furthest distances is Beyer1999; Aggarwal2001 shows it depends on the distance metric.
Dimensionality itself poses a further difficulty. \citet{Beyer1999} show that as the number of dimensions grows, the distances to the nearest and to the furthest
point become increasingly similar, so that pairwise distances become progressively less discriminative. Molecular representations are routinely high-dimensional and sparse, and when a
large proportion of their dimensions carry no information relevant to the property being modeled, distances computed over them are dominated by those uninformative dimensions.


\section{Noise in molecular data}
% [notes moved to thesis_notes_moved.md]

Real-world cheminformatics datasets contain label noise arising from multiple sources across the drug discovery pipeline. Although noise is often modeled as being random, or drawn from a Gaussian distribution, other varieties of noise must be considered when working with experimental data. To properly model experimental noise, we must first understand where the noise is coming from.

% moved here from the start of the Models section (2026-09-30)
% [CLAUDE 2026-10-09] citation fix: Kolmar2021 names GPs and conformal prediction, not BNNs.
One key issue with QSAR models is the standard ML assumption that labels represent true values, as most chemical datasets lack sufficient replications to provide a robust statistical representation of the underlying distributions \citep{Kolmar2021}. A single measurement, or even multiple measurements for a given label, will not guarantee that this label accurately reflects the population mean \citep{Kolmar2021}. Most ML models rely on the assumption that the training data is, in fact, an accurate reflection of the population. Therefore, they treat these potentially noisy labels as discrete quantities rather than distributions, which opens the door to overfitting to noise. However, Bayesian methods such as Gaussian processes (GPs), and conformal prediction, do not make this assumption \citep{Kolmar2021}.

% [CLAUDE 2026-10-09] citation fix: Kruger2012's Laplace fit is to differences between orthologs and paralogs; repeat-assay differences are only said to resemble them.
% [CLAUDE 2026-10-09] citation fix: Kalliokoski2013: 9, 10 and 8 of 10 inspected pairs; the paper says 'in most cases'.
Studies involving noise, and specifically the quantification of noise robustness, typically approach label noise by adding independent, zero-mean, Gaussian error to the labels. However, there's evidence to suggest that noise from experimental assays takes on different forms. \citet{Kruger2012} rejected normality of the differences in binding affinity between orthologous targets by Anderson-Darling test, found them approximately Laplace, and found that differences between assays of the same ligand and target closely resemble them. \citet{Kalliokoski2013} examined the widest disagreements among 16,844 pairs of repeat pIC$_{50}$ measurements and found that in most cases, the wide gap between repeat values was due to annotation error rather than to an imprecise assay. 

% [CLAUDE 2026-10-09] citation fix: Heid2023 injected the nitrogen-dependent noise on purpose; it is not an observed measurement effect.
Although numerous studies have explored the effects of noise on ML algorithms for molecular property prediction, comparisons between different molecular representations remain limited. \citet{Kolmar2021} examined the predictive limits of QSAR in the presence of noise, showing that by learning the underlying trends rather than fitting to noise, the models can achieve predictions more accurate than those of their training data. They assumed experimental noise could be modeled as Gaussian-distributed, using the Central Limit Theorem to argue that aggregated experimental measurements from multiple independent error sources exhibit a Gaussian distribution. However, \citet{Heid2023} distinguish random noise, which affects all molecules alike, from systematic noise that differs between regions of chemical space. Using synthetic noise applied only to nitrogen-containing molecules, they showed that mean-variance estimation can detect it.

% [CLAUDE 2026-10-09] citation fix: Bentz2013's 62% is for the cell-line laboratories only (11 + 6 + 4), digoxin efflux ratio, Table 7.
Beyond just the distribution, the assumptions of independence and zero mean may fail as well. \citet{Bentz2013} examined P-glycoprotein transport measurements from 23 laboratories and found that, among the laboratories using cell lines, 62\% of the variance in log digoxin efflux ratio was due to differences between laboratories. When you pool data from different sources, each source has the potential to carry a shared offset. These sources usually contain families of related compounds, breaking the independence assumption. The zero-mean assumption is not assured either, especially when true values fall above or below an assay's detection limit, called censoring. \citet{Svensson2025} count censored labels in 15 industrial assays, of which 13 carry censored labels and eight sit between a quarter and two thirds censored. Chapter~\ref{chap:robustness} tests noise conditions that vary the distribution, the correlation, and the bias of injected error separately, at a matched quantity of noise for every condition except censoring.

