We compare the variance decomposition on both clean and noisy labels (Figure~\ref{fig:three_outcomes}a). For predictive performance, the artificial label noises lowers the impact of the choice of model family, while the replicate and residual take more. This means that the variance is left more up to chance and to the underlying variation within the data itself with label noise, which is the expected behavior. Robustness divides the variance a bit differently. Representation explains 8.1\% of the variance in \aucnorm, against 31.1\% of clean $R^2$, while model family rises to 47.2\%. The replicate nearly disappears from \aucnorm, likely because each replicate's curve is divided by that replicate's own clean $R^2$, so a difficult split lowers both and cancels out.

We include two terms which stem from the data rather than the model, the residual and replicate terms. The replicate measures how much of the variance can be attributed to the train and test split, which in this case is a scaffold split. On QM9 we replicated each experiment 10 times with different random draws of $N=10{,}000$, each time giving a new scaffold split. On the assay datasets, a replicate refers to one of the five scaffold CV folds. A large replicate term in ANOVA indicates that the folds differ from one another. The residual is variance that no other term accounts for.

