\section{Predictive performance and noise robustness}

The variance decomposition tells us what aspect of QSAR modeling affects predictive performance and robustness, however it does not tell us about the characteristics of individual models. All model's performance degrades as the labels get increasingly corrupted with noise (Figure~\ref{fig:curves}). The loss remains small until the artificial noise reaches about half the spread of the clean training labels, and then it begins to drop. The rate it drops and the quantity of noise retained is not necessarily correlated to the model's predictive performance on clean labels. Across the 13 base models under Gaussian noise at ECFP4, clean $R^2$ and \aucnorm{} rank in opposite directions at a Spearman correlation of $-0.10$ ($p = 0.73$) (Figure~\ref{fig:decoupling}). If anything, the lower a model's predictive performance on clean labels, the less it has to lose as the labels get noisier.

The link between predictive performance and noise robustness can be examined by model family (Figure~\ref{fig:decoupling}). The forests, boosted trees, SVM, and GP all retain relatively high predictive performance on clean labels while maintaining relatively strong robustness to noise. The plain NNs and BNNs are among the highest in predictive performance on clean labels, and are the four least robust. The two variational networks sit between these groups, with lower predictive performance on clean labels and middling noise robustness. Standard feed-forward networks can make strong predictions under clean labels, however they are not a safe bet under noisy ones.

NGBoost is a standout case in the sense it is highly dependent on its paired molecular representation. Under Gaussian noise it has the highest \aucnorm{} on every one of the six representations, yet also the lowest in predictive performance on four of the six representations. It is one of the worst performing models with respect to $R^2$ on clean data. Its high robustness is partially due to the fact that when you start with a low predictive performance, you don't have as much to lose. However, it maintains shallow robustness curves across representations. On ECFP4 NGBoost's predictive performance ranks lowest up to a noise level of 0.75 and stays in the lower half at every noise level. However, on PDV it starts second lowest and ends highest, surpassing the other models between noise levels 1.0 and 1.5. So when paired with the right features, NGBoost has the potential to make better predictions under higher quantities of label noise. On group-shifted noise, RF has higher \aucnorm{} values than NGBoost on ECFP4, Avalon and ChemBERTa. Even then, NGBoost reports the highest noise robustness for PDV, MHG-GNN and Sort \& Slice. While NGBoost performs well under higher label noise, LightGBM performs well under smaller quantities of noise. Its performance drops sharply after noise level 1.0.

As we've seen, the choice of representation can have an impact on noise robustness, but that impact is determined by model family, represented by the interaction term between model and representation in Figure~\ref{fig:grid}. Some models are more impacted by representation than others, though. In general, networks span a wider range of \aucnorm{} across the six representations than any of the other models. Between ECFP4 and PDV under Gaussian noise, the four plain and Bayesian networks differ by between 0.037 and 0.065 of \aucnorm, while no tree or kernel model shifts by more than 0.011. VBLLs are an exception, as no two variational networks differ by more than 0.019.

