Across all datasets, models struggle to fit with group-shifted noise the most, producing lower \aucnorm{} values across the board. For grouped-shifted, RF is the dominant model on hERG $K_i$ and logD, where it ranks first or second for \aucnorm{} with every representation. For predictive performance at under higher label noise (noise level 1.0), which is more impacted by the clean label performance, SVM ranks first to third on logD while RF ranks fifth to ninth across datasets. Both are strong contenders for systematic label noise.

LightGBM has particularly varied results across the datasets. On LogD, it has relatively high predictive performance and middle-of-the-pack noise robustness. However, on hERG and Caco-2, it loses the most with increased noise, particularly on Gaussian and grouped-shifted noise. The effects are not as pronounced on grouped-wider, although it still ranks among the least robust there.  XGBoost degrades in the same way on hERG and Caco-2.

While the results are not identical between the datasets, there are some patterns that emerge (Figure~\ref{fig:transfer}). In general, the relative \aucnorm{} model rankings match between QM9 and logD, as well as between Caco-2 and hERG $K_i$ with every representation except ChemBERTa.  RF is the most consistent, among the 13 base models under Gaussian noise it ranks between first and seventh for \aucnorm{} on every dataset and representation, second or third on QM9 and first on logD at five of six. The GP has the highest predictive performance on clean labels on all three assay datasets with nearly every representation, and ranks third to ninth for \aucnorm. NGBoost, the most robust model on QM9, ranks first to third on logD, second to tenth on Caco-2 and sixth to ninth on hERG $K_i$. LightGBM falls furthest, ranking 10th to 13th on Caco-2 and hERG $K_i$ at every representation while being among the highest in predictive performance on clean labels. VBLL-$\alpha$ is the opposite, ranking first or second for \aucnorm{} on hERG
$K_i$ and last on logD at every representation, from one of the lowest clean $R^2$ values. This fits the pattern of the NNs being exceptionally varied.

We consider the impact of label noise on each model family. While the forests, RF and QRF, are typically middle-ground in terms of predictive performance on clean labels, they are the most reliably robust to noise across all datasets. Their rank barely changes between noise conditions, so there is no pattern of noise they are better suited to. Caco-2 is the exception, where they rank third to seventh rather than first or second.

Other models perform conditionally well, and in some cases that condition is the representation they are paired with. The GP has some of the higher predictive capabilities on clean labels and under Gaussian noise across assay datasets. It is the highest on logD and Caco-2 under grouped-wider noise as well, but under grouped-shifted noise it falls. The choice of representation has little impact on GP, moving its \aucnorm{} less than repeating the split does on every dataset.

There is a slight consideration with using both \aucnorm{} and predictive performance side-by-side. \aucnorm{} measures the fraction of its own clean $R^2$ that a model keeps, so predictive performance on clean labels works against it by construction. A model that already predicts poorly has little to lose and often scores a higher \aucnorm, while a model that starts with strong performance has more to lose. The GP and NGBoost are the clearest cases. The GP has among the highest predictive performance on clean labels and a middling \aucnorm, while NGBoost has the highest \aucnorm{} across the board from one of the lowest clean $R^2$ values. While NGBoost's predictive performance is highly dependent on its representation, its robustness is not. On QM9 it is both the most robust model and the lowest ranked model for predictive performance up to a noise level of 0.75 with every representation. But with PDV it's able to retain some predictive performance under noise, finishing highest across all the models at noise level 1.5.

Upon first glance, SVM does not seem like a standout, however, it is the model whose standing is better with non-Gaussian noise. Among RF, the GP, NGBoost and SVM, the four models run under every condition, SVM has the lowest \aucnorm{} of the four under Gaussian noise on almost every dataset and representation, and rises to second under Laplace, Student-$t$ and outlier noise and under grouped-wider noise. Gaussian noise is actually the one condition it is worst at. Its \aucnorm{} stays in the middle, partially because it starts high, and it ranks among the five highest for predictive performance at a noise level on every assay dataset under every noise condition.

The feed-forward networks are the most variable, although in general they have some of the highest predictive performance under clean labels and are generally not robust to noise. They are also dependent on the choice of representation. VBLL networks perform poorly on clean labels, are the least robust to noise on logD, yet are the most robust on Caco-2 and hERG $K_i$. The gradient-boosted trees, LightGBM and XGBoost, were also quite variable, with strong predictive performance on clean labels and poor noise robustness on Caco-2 and hERG $K_i$.

The quantity of noise is an important factor as well. At a noise level of 0.5 the models still rank by predictive performance much as they do on clean labels, while by 1.0 the order has changed, most of all on hERG $K_i$ and then QM9. The order of models by \aucnorm{} is broadly similar across noise conditions, with the forests near the top under each.

While it is clear that QSAR model performance drops with label noise, different models drop faster than others. How much noise a dataset tolerates before anything happens is itself a property of the dataset. Up to a noise level of 0.3, three tenths of the spread of the clean labels, the models are largely unaffected by the noise on the HOMO--LUMO gap and on logD, where every base model with ECFP4 still keeps at least 93\% of its clean $R^2$; on Caco-2 one of the 13 and on hERG $K_i$ four of the 13 are already below 90\% at that level. By a noise level of 0.5 the lowest retained fraction is 90\% on the HOMO--LUMO gap against 76\% on hERG $K_i$ (Figure~\ref{fig:curves_all}). Boosted tree ensembles are some of the first to fall. On Caco-2 with ECFP4, LightGBM has among the highest predictive performance on clean labels. It is also the first tree ensemble to fall below 90\% of that performance, at a noise level of 0.5. By level 1.5 it retains 26\% of its original $R^2$, compared to 63\% for RF on ECFP4. XGBoost drops off at the next recorded noise level. However, NGBoost is the opposite, having some of the lowest predictive performance on clean labels and the flattest curve with injected label noise across all models. On the HOMO--LUMO gap it never falls below 90\% of its clean $R^2$ at any level tested. The networks drop at an inconsistent rate. VBLL-$\alpha$ is the earliest to fall on logD, dropping below 90\% of its clean $R^2$ at a level of 0.5 and reaching 38\% by 1.5. Yet on Caco-2 and hERG $K_i$ it is among the flattest models.

