\subsection{Aleatoric and epistemic attribution}

While this tells us that the models are reportedly less certain under noisy data, we don't know what the model is attributing this uncertainty to. To answer this question, we decompose uncertainty estimates into their aleatoric and epistemic components wherever possible. We expect to see aleatoric uncertainty rise with injected label noise, while the epistemic component should remain relatively constant, as it is the aleatoric component that describes uncertainty in the underlying data. The models whose frameworks allowed for this decomposition include full-BNNs with a variance head, the heteroscedastic GP, and QRF (Table~\ref{tab:uncertainty_rise}). The two heteroscedastic VBLL networks also report both components, and they separate them more often than any other model, though not on every dataset and representation. They are not drawn in the figures, as they were not scored out of fold.

The full-BNNs with a variance head behave as expected (Figure~\ref{fig:decomposition}), the epistemic component grows by at most 0.43 of the aleatoric growth in every configuration where the ratio can be computed. Despite QRF's strong predictive performance and noise robustness, both of its aleatoric and epistemic components rise with noise and on some representations, the epistemic component actually rises at a faster rate.

Unlike the predictive performance and robustness, the representation actually dictates whether decomposition works. For QRF, the epistemic growth is 0.61 to 1.14 times the aleatoric growth on the three fingerprints, 1.02 to 1.44 times on PDV, and 1.29 to 2.04 times on ChemBERTa and MHG-GNN. Under Gaussian noise the heteroscedastic GP keeps its epistemic growth below a quarter of its aleatoric growth on PDV, ChemBERTa and MHG-GNN except MHG-GNN on QM9, but not on ECFP4 under grouped-shifted noise, where the two grow by similar amounts.

The noise conditions follow the pattern found in robustness, the three conditions which alternate the noise distribution show similar decompositions to those under Gaussian noise, while group-shifted raises the epistemic share of the heteroscedastic GP and of QRF in every pairing of dataset and representation. This scaffold family-based offset may be appearing as structure in the data rather than label noise to the models.

