The aleatoric component indicates that label noise increased, but it doesn't tell us by how much (Figure~\ref{fig:aleatoric_injected}). For each unit of added noise it rises by roughly 0.4 to 1 unit, depending on model, dataset and representation. The aleatoric component can therefore compare noise between datasets or noise levels, but it does not estimate a dataset's internal error.

Uncertainty estimation breaks under censoring as the process of clipping the labels narrows their spread, which in turn shrinks the total uncertainty. None of the models tested treat a clipped label as a noisy one, so they actually grow more confident while losing information.

