\subsection{Molecules with identical feature vectors}

A standard fingerprint- or descriptor-based representation can produce the same feature vector for two separate molecules. In turn, models using this representation will produce a single prediction for the molecules with identical features. Identical feature vectors do not always mean that the representation has merged two different molecules, since a dataset pooled from several sources can hold two measurements of the same molecule.

Many fingerprints are computed without stereochemistry \citep{orsi2024map4c}. In this research, ECFP4 is built with stereochemistry off, and the 2D descriptor sets (PDV, Mordred) also ignore it, so almost every molecule they produce identical representations for is a stereoisomer of another (Table~\ref{tab:flaws:ties}). Any representation that reads from these subsequently merges stereoisomers. Additionally, methods using the Weisfeiler--Lehman relabeling scheme are bounded by the 1-Weisfeiler--Lehman test (Chapter~\ref{chap:background}), so some non-isomorphic graphs also receive the same vector. This is seen in the graph-kernel features which tie 105 of 1,412 hERG $K_i$ molecules, 56 of which differ only in stereochemistry and 49 in more than that. 

Whether or not a learned embedding is able to differentiate stereoisomers is dependent on its input as well as its individual implementation. SMI-TED, a pretrained SMILES encoder, ties 56 hERG $K_i$, 633 LogD and 177 Caco-2 molecules, all stereoisomers. A tokenizer can drop stereochemistry prior to training. The tokenizer of the ChemBERTa-77M-MTR checkpoint (ChemBERTa-2 in Table~\ref{tab:constant_features}, called ChemBERTa in Chapter~\ref{chap:robustness}) gives 72 of the 1,415 hERG $K_i$ molecules of Chapter~\ref{chap:robustness} the same token sequence as another molecule. Since stereoisomers can differ in measured activity \citep{Nguyen2006}, these representations lack potentially useful chemical information. 

