On a single dataset, often very little of a standard representation varies. On hERG $K_i$, 1,815 of the 2,048 ECFP4 features take one value in at least 95\% of the 1,412 molecules (Section~\ref{sec:flaws:constant}). Several representations also give different molecules, mostly stereoisomers, the same feature vector, and no model can separate those molecules, whatever their labels. ECFP4 gives 56 of the 1,412 hERG $K_i$ molecules a vector shared with another molecule, all of them stereoisomers (``Molecules with identical feature vectors'').

A distance sums over every feature, so when only a few features matter for the property, the rest dominate the sum (Section~\ref{sec:flaws:similarity}). Across 70 combinations of dataset and representation, the Spearman correlation between pair distance and label difference was below 0.10 in 44. % CHECK: NOTES say keep the all-pairs vs nearest-neighbor contrast only if the full runs support it. The numbers below come from distance.tex, computed on the saved features, not from the full runs.
Predicting each label from its five nearest neighbors gave a higher Spearman correlation in all 70, so the useful information sits in a few close pairs rather than in the distance as a whole.

