\section{Introduction}

So far, Chapter~\ref{chap:flaws} explained how the standard methods of estimating molecular similarity, or conversely distance are flawed and potentially break the SPP. Specifically, the representaions used in these similarity calculationsc contains irrelevant features that either do not vary or more generally do not pertain to the prediction task at hand. This chapter explores a solution. With the understanding that each representation contains a set of features which contain a certain type of information about that data. We aim to select the most informative features across a larger set of representations, while filtering out the irrelevant ones. We create an adaptive hybrid representation, a set of features bounded by a budget that is selected from several base representations by a search using one dataset and downstream model. The goal is to select a set of features that is customized for a given model and prediction task. As discussed in Chapter~\ref{chap:robustness}, performance can vary signficantly across datasets and models. Rather than hope the features you give a model will be useful, this process let's you prescreen them. 


In this chapter we build the adaptive hybrid representation, referred to as the hybrid representation, first reporting on when and why it helps. The questions this chapter and Chapter~\ref{chap:similarity} address are set out in Chapter~\ref{chap:intro} (Section~\ref{sec:intro:hybrid_questions}). This chapter measures predictive performance under both standard labels and labels with injected artificial noise. Distance meseasures between hybrids, noise detection and LNL are covered in Chapter~\ref{chap:similarity}.


\subsection{Combining representations}
