Not every molecular representation is equal. In fact, they hold very different types of information. RDKit2D descriptors matched or outperformed features derived from embedded GNNs and pretrained language models on many datasets across 62,820 models on a variety of molecular property prediction benchmarks \citep{Deng2023}. Descriptor-based models held 73\% of top-three positions on 11 benchmarks \citep{Jiang2021}. In 1,310 ChEMBL assays, feedforward networks on ECFP fingerprints outperformed graph convolutions and recurrent networks trained on SMILES \citep{Mayr2018}. We know that the choice of representation matters, as is discussed in Chapter~\ref{chap:robustness}, yet this choice is usually decided by trial-and-error. Beyond just molecules, general protein language models predict antibody expression better than antibody-specific ones, while the antibody-specific models are better on binding \citep{Kenlay2024}, and no pathology foundation model is best on every clinical task \citep{ElNahhas2025}. We turn our attention to combining features from these different representations. 

Most research in this field relies on a single representation, and few combine features from different representations beyond concatenation. One of the reasons just pooling features from different representations together doesn't work is dimensionality. If you add a standard ECFP4 ($d=2,048$) with Morderd descriptors ($d=1,613$) and an MHG-GNN embedding ($d=1,024$), you end up with 4,685 features, which is far too many considering many datasets from experimental assays contain only a few hundred labeled samples. With such ratios, more features mean weaker predictions \citep{Hughes1968, Bellman1961}. Therefore, we consider ways of combining representations. 

