The most straightforward method of combining representations is concatenation. This however degrades wiht non-homogenous feature sets and obscures which reprsentation is responsible for the score \citep{Stahlschmidt2022}. \citet{Deng2023} found that appending RDKit2D descriptors to GROVER lowered RMSE on 9 of 24 datasets tested. Late fusion trains one model per source and aggregates the predictions \citep{Steyaert2023}. MOGONET is of this kind, combining per-source predictions in label space \citep{Wang2021}. While this is effective, it loses interactions between the source representations, an issue which is addresed by intermediate fusion, for example cross-attention between sources \citep{Beaude2025}. Multiple kernel learning does this with kernels rather than whole models \citep{Gonen2011, Cichonska2018}, and produces no interpretable feature sets. \citet{Laufktter2019} found that combining bioactivity-based fingerprints with structure-based ones like ECFP4 improved both predictive performance and scaffold-hopping. Similarly, \citet{Turkina2025} combined features from six non-hashed fingerprints with feature importance scores and showed that the additional features consistently improved predictive accuracy over the original fingerprints across a range of models. Most of this work involves limitation combinations, adding together standard fingerprints and linear representations \citep{Xie2020, Laufktter2019}. Although the features derived from embeddings are not as conceptually-straightforward, they contain another axis of information. 

Beyond simply combining features, there are a range of other methods that handle training on multiple representatinos or excessive feature sets. Ensembling fits one model per representation and aggregats their predictions, essentially combining the information carried by each representation after tarining rather than before. Consensus QSAR works this way \citep{Valsecchi2020}. One drawback is that no model in an ensemble sees features from other representations so their interaction is never measure, and every representation and every model must be computed for each new 

When you want to select filter a feature set for the most important to the prediction task, you can use a dimensionality reduction technique or feature selection methodology. Principal component analysis (PCA) uses linear combinations of the original features, ordered by the variance they explain \citep{Jolliffe2016}. Their combined features are dependant on the original feature units unless each feature is z-scored first which matters when data from different modalities like fingerprint bits, descriptors and embeddings are joined \citep{Jolliffe2016}. PCA never sees the labels, and components with small variance can still be important in prediction \citep{Jolliffe1982}. Partial least squares (PLS), the supervised counterpart in chemometrics, builds components that covary with the property \citep{Wold2001}. Neither gives components that can be traced to one substructure, descriptor or representation, making them difficult to interpret.

Feature selection keeps a subset of the original features, unlike PCA or PLS which builds new ones. Of its three families \citep{Guyon2003}, filter methods score features against the labels before any model is fitted, as minimum-redundancy maximum-relevance selection (mRMR) ranks features by their mutual information, defined as the label minus its redundancy with already-chosen features \citep{Peng2005}. Wrapper methods score subsets of features by fitting the model \citep{Kohavi1997}. Embedded methods select features while the model is fitted, as the does lasso \citep{Tibshirani1996}. Recursive feature elimination repeatedly fits a model and drops the lowest-ranked features \citep{Guyon2002}.

