\subsection{From base representations to a hybrid}\label{sec:method:build}

The input to the search is $K$ frozen representation matrices $\mathbf{X}^{(k)} \in \mathbb{R}^{n\times d_k}$, $k = 1, \dots, K$, for the same $n$ samples, whose widths $d_k$ range from tens to thousands of features, as well as a label vector $\mathbf{y}$, one group label per sample for cross-validation (for example, its scaffold group), the evaluation metric, the task type, one or more downstream models and a list of budgets. It returns a hybrid of at most $B$ features before any augmentation features added outside the budget (Section~\ref{sec:method:augmentation}), where the budget $B$ is a maximum rather than a target, and a blueprint that records the settings necessary to rebuild the hybrid representation on another set of features, presumably another subset within the same dataset.

% TODO: double check this is an accurate description of what's happening with large reps - which method???
When combining representations, we are careful to avoid data leakage. Because the feature selection process trains on the features and their labels, pooling all the features prior to selection would leak validation-level information into the training, which is a well-documented bias in feature selection  \citep{Ambroise2002, Vabalas2019}. As such, we run each hybrid search within a cross-validation fold. When a single representation is score, we impute missing values with training-fold medians. A representation wider than 5,000 features is scored on its own using only its top 500 features (200 on datasets under 1,000 samples). These are chosen in each fold by a LightGBM model fitted on the training part. This affects only that representation's score, not the features the hybrid can select from it.

The data are split at three levels within CV runs. Every split keeps each group whole, a scaffold for small molecules, a sequence cluster for antibodies, and some pre-defined grouping for datasets from other domains (Section~\ref{sec:method:datasets}).
With fewer than five groups, the inner and internal splits use as many folds as there are groups (Additional file~\ref{app:add:fold_counts}). Figure~\ref{fig:split_levels} shows the three levels as nested boxes.

