To run a hybrid search, we first measure feature importance, select how many features to take from each contributing base representation, and how to score a candidate feature set. And for each of those steps, there's no one right way to do it. Different prediction tasks prefer different methods of scoring feature importance or candidate features, different sets of input hybrids, and so forth. The search tries to find the ideal settings for the hybrid feature selection process, tailor to given dataset and downsream model. All combinations of settings values are explored, as the settings interact. The best budget depends on the downstream model, as shown below for nanobody thermostability. This search grid also tests the chapter's claim that no single setting suits every dataset.

Six settings in total are searched, namely the downstream model, the budget, the representation count, the importance method, the allocation method and the internal evaluator. Table~\ref{tab:search_tiers} gives default search settings, organised by runtime speed, and Table~\ref{tab:decided_grid} the smaller set searched on each dataset in this chapter (Section~\ref{sec:method:decided_grid}).  The importance methods tested are LightGBM gain, TreeSHAP or DeepLIFT (Section~\ref{sec:method:importance}). Feature-level importance depends on the model as well as on the data \citep{Dong2020}, and as such we observe that within the search, a different ranking of features produces a different hybrid output.

The downstream model is trained on the hybrid representation. This model can be a supervised model of any form, classification or regression. We used random forest, XGBoost, LightGBM, SVM, a deep neural network (DNN) and a Gaussian process. Each dataset in this chapter uses two of them (Section~\ref{sec:method:models}). 

The budget is the maximum number of features in the hybrid, taken from eight values (10, 25, 50, 100, 250, 500, 1,000 and 2,000 features). When the budget is too small, informative features can be left out, yet when it is too large it will pick up on too many uninformative ones, especially ones that give small datasets room to overfit. 

