We select a set of up to 10 base representations per dataset, chosen prior to the search (Table~\ref{tab:dataset_reps}). The selections were made based on each representation's relative performance on the prediction task. We took into account what type of information each representation contained, and tried to span a wide set across the selected 10. For example, while there are many high-performing graph-based embeddings in QSAR modelling, for the purposes of testing a feature selection process which takes features from different sources of information, it is better to have a mix of graph-based embeddings, as well as other types of molecular representations like substructure fingerprints, property vectors, sequential embeddings, etc.  The representation count (2, 3, 5 or all) sets how many may contribute features, keeping those with the highest standalone scores on each fold. The number of representation counts to test is searched in the sweep as datasets differ in how many representations have features worth selecting, and pooled feature selection over all representations has no such setting. While we tested various methods of pooling the features, we found computing feature importance scores and concatenation techniques on a global pooled feature set was far less effective than when the feature scoring is kept on a per-representation basis. Since the features contain different types of information at different scales, pooling the features too early makes comparisons within in the pool challenging. We found that it's better to determine which features are important to each representation first, and go from there.

