\subsubsection{Feature importance scoring}

We score run a feature importance scoring method on each base representation which gives each features a single positive score. These scores remain relative within a given representation, so they are not compared or normalised between representations.  Split-based tree importance is biased toward features with many possible split points \citep{Strobl2007}, which matters here because we use binary fingerprint bits alongside continuous embeddings. No single importance method works best across datasets and downstream models. After testing a wider variety of methods found across literature, we settled three methods whose rankings did not correlate strongly with each other and whose selected features performed well as model input (Additional file~\ref{app:add:design_experiments}).

We score features using LightGBM gain importance, SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) values for tree ensembles (TreeSHAP) and DeepLIFT (Deep Learning Important FeaTures) \citep{Shrikumar2017}. LightGBM gain measures how much a feature reduced the training loss where trees split on it \citep{ke2017lightgbm}. TreeSHAP gives each feature's exact Shapley value for each molecule's prediction from a tree model \citep{Lundberg2020}. DeepLIFT measures how much a feature moves the output of a small network, trained with Adam \citep{Kingma2015}, away from its output on an all-zero reference input, which after standardization places every feature at its training mean \citep{Shrikumar2017, Kokhlikyan2020, Ancona2018}. On Caco-2, the median share of features scoring above 0 was 30\% for LightGBM gain, 70\% for TreeSHAP and 90\% for DeepLIFT (preliminary, over 10 representations and 3 folds). Table~\ref{tab:importance_methods} gives the settings of each method, including the pre-screen of representations wider than 2,000 features. Each dataset searches one, two or all three importance methods depending on its individually-decided search settings (Table~\ref{tab:decided_grid}).

