\subsubsection{Feature allocation methods}\label{sec:method:allocation}

The allocation method decides how many of the $B$ features each representation contributes while the importance method decides which. Of the five methods searched, four give each representation a fixed share computed from its rescaled standalone score (linear, squared, softmax and rank-decay allocation). The fifth, the global pool, lets the candidate features offered by every representation compete in one importance ranking.

The four fixed-share methods compute importance shares from the standalone score on internal validation. Only linear allocation makes the share proportional to that score. All four assume that representations with stronger predictive performance contribute more valuable features. This assumption fails when a strong representation relies on a few select features. In that case, a large share of features will be given to that representation, picking up on weaker features and potentially missing out on stronger ones from other representations. Fixed shares ignore complementarity, so two representations carrying the same information both receive a share. Only the global pool ranks features from different representations against each other, however ranking feature importance on a global pool is dubious as the feautures are not necessarily appropriate to compare. 

The first method of feature allocation we tried was a greedy forward selection, in which the hybrid is build by one block of features at a time  \citep{Kohavi1997, Guyon2003}. In each round, the next block is added from representations in order of wihch improves the internal validation score. This in theory lets it choose features for how well they work together. In practice, this is very expensive. With blocks of 10 features and a budget of 500 and 10 representations, it needs up to 500 model fits per configuration and fold against 10 for a score-based method. It was also less accurate: in preliminary runs on Jain antibody FcRn retention it placed 16th of 17 allocation methods, with a Spearman correlation of 0.3600 against 0.4526 for linear allocation. We therefore removed it from the search (Additional file~\ref{app:add:design_experiments}).

