\subsubsection{Search configuration and internal evaluation}

The most useful features for an RF may not be the most useful for an SVM, and vice versa. The relative importance of a feature is dependent on the model \citep{Dong2020}. Within the search grid, we us an internal evaluator to fit on one candidate feature set at a time. This process is separate from the final downstream model evaluation. In a standard search, four internal evaluators are used: RF, DNN, LightGBM, and the matched evaluator. When the internal evaluator is set to \texttt{match}, the search scores each candidate hybrid on the same model type as the downstream model, for each of the twelve model types used in this thesis. This means that internal evaluators outside the standard sweep may be tested.  For a downstream model outside of the pre-defined set, if no matched evaluator exists in the code, and the search uses the other internal evaluators that were requested. Each internal evaluator is fitted on the internal training fold and scores on the internal validation fold within that CV iteration using the dataset's specified evaluation metric. 

