A random forest, LightGBM or DNN downstream model is searched with three internal evaluators, because its matched evaluator is already one of the other three. An XGBoost, SVM or Gaussian process downstream model is searched with four, because the matched evaluator adds one of its own family. Only the DNN evaluator shares its downstream model's architecture and training settings, and even it draws its 20\% early-stopping molecules at random, whereas the downstream DNN splits them by the outer split's groups. Additional file~\ref{app:add:model_settings} gives every evaluator setting.

The internal evaluator's scores determine how the budget is split and which representations are kept. The internal evaluator ranks candidate feature sets, it does explicity not rank features within a representation. This process of selecting a feature set by fitting a model on it, and keeping the set that scores the best is known as the wrapper method \citep{Kohavi1997}. This is distinct from a filter method which scores features without fitting a model. 

