\subsubsection{Adaptive pruning}\label{sec:method:pruning}
% TODO: review, needs manual review -- rewritten 2026-10-08 as a short subsection; the long version with Table tab:pruning_rules is in thesis.tex.bak_before_ch5_review_2026-10-08. Rules from the stored schedule in src/kirby/find_best_hybrid.py:3824-3925; the 79 and 44 hours and the "never removed the best" calibration come from the old text, not re-checked.
The search also has an adaptive pruning option, which we did not use in this research: because of time constraints, the per-dataset cuts above, which remove far more configurations, were used instead. Pruning scores every configuration on the first two of the five folds and stops scoring those that are clearly behind. Budgets are scored from smallest to largest, and once every budget up to 100 features is scored, pairs of allocation and importance method with no configuration in the better half of their group are not searched at larger budgets. Each remaining setting is then voted on within groups of configurations that share every other setting, and a value is removed if it is among the best in too few groups. The survivors are scored on the other three folds, and the best is picked as without pruning. Unlike the cuts above, pruning decides from the dataset's own fold scores during the search, so it needs no earlier search. Its cut-offs were set on five datasets so that it never removed the best configuration, and it cut the estimated run time on LogD from 79 to 44 hours. Successive halving \citep{Jamieson2016} and Hyperband \citep{Li2018Hyperband} likewise drop configurations after partial evaluations.

