This experiment asks whether the gain comes from drawing on several representations or only from having fewer features. On regression datasets, every method receives $B$ features from all base representations joined, fitted on the fold's training samples. The methods are the two best hybrids at budget $B$ from the fold's search, mRMR \citep{Peng2005}, recursive feature elimination \citep{Guyon2002} and $B$ random features. The comparison with PCA at the same width is the one described above. mRMR ranks features by mutual information and stops at 200 features for run time. Every small-molecule budget is 250 features or more, so mRMR has no result on any small-molecule dataset. Recursive elimination fits a 50-tree random forest of depth 5 and removes 10\% of the features at each step. Each of the dataset's two downstream models scores every method, so only the features differ.

