\paragraph{Stability and blueprints.}
% TODO: manual review -- kept from the old text (Nogueira2018), with the varying set sizes added 2026-10-09 (c24 n_features_selected_per_fold differs by fold)
Selection on each of the $F$ folds gives a binary vector $\mathbf{z}_f \in \{0,1\}^d$, where $d$ is the number of features in all base representations joined, not the size of the union of the selected features. We measure the stability of the selection with the index of \citet{Nogueira2018},
\[
\hat\Phi = 1 - \frac{\frac1d\sum_{f=1}^d s_f^2}{\frac{\bar k}{d}\left(1-\frac{\bar k}{d}\right)}, \qquad s_f^2 = \frac{F}{F-1}\hat p_f(1-\hat p_f).
\]
% TODO: manual review -- kept, last sentence added 2026-10-09
Here $\hat p_f$ is the fraction of folds that selected feature $f$, and $\bar k$ is the mean number of features selected. $\hat\Phi=1$ means the same features on every fold, and $\hat\Phi \approx 0$ is what random selection gives. Unstable feature sets would weaken any chemical reading of the blueprints. Each fold picks its own budget, so the sets compared can differ in size, and the index allows for this through $\bar k$. To read a blueprint, each selected feature is traced to its base representation, and its importance in the fitted model is measured with SHAP \citep{Lundberg2020} on tree models, with the DeepLIFT-based SHAP explainer on the DNN, and with Kernel SHAP on 50 held-out samples for the SVM and the Gaussian process.
