A separate local experiment on the same three folds built the hybrid with 48 allocation settings. It crosses four ways of weighting the representations (linear, squared, softmax and rank decay) with two ways of taking features. Under the first, each representation contributes its weighted share from the top of its own ranking. Under the second, each representation offers candidates in proportion to its weight to a pool of twice the budget, and the budget is filled from one LightGBM gain ranking of the pool. Squared weights with a joint ranking is the global pool of Table~\ref{tab:alloc_methods}; the other three weightings with a joint ranking were run only in this experiment. Features are ranked by LightGBM gain on each fold's training molecules. Each representation's weight is computed from its score with the downstream model over three inner folds of the training molecules. Each setting draws on the two, three or five representations that score best with the downstream model on each fold's training molecules (Table~\ref{tab:prelim_variants_subset_reps}), at 500 and 1,000 features. The internal evaluator is the downstream model itself (the matched evaluator), whereas the default hybrid uses a random forest. Unlike the default hybrid, these hybrids fill the budget with features of zero importance, so each holds exactly the budget on every fold. None of the 48 settings is the default hybrid, which uses linear weights and its own ranking at 250 features; its scores are in Table~\ref{tab:prelim_budget}.

