% TODO: review, needs manual review
% [CLAUDE 2026-10-09] citation fix: 'median error 11 out of 40' is not in Ambroise2002 (its numbers: near zero outside, 0.40-0.45 inside); 'median 11' is Simon et al. 2003 and is 11 of 20.
% [CLAUDE 2026-10-09] citation fix: Cawley2010 does not argue that feature selection is model selection with a complexity cost.
\citet{Ambroise2002} showed the extreme case of overfitting through feature selection. On colon-cancer data with permuted class labels, an SVM with recursive feature elimination reached a cross-validated error close to zero when genes were selected on the full dataset first. With selection redone inside each fold, the error was between 0.40 and 0.45, close to chance. The bias from pre-selection can exceed that from hyperparameter tuning \citep{Vabalas2019}. \citet{Cawley2010} showed that over-fitting the model-selection criterion biases performance estimates, and called this bias analogous to the feature-selection bias of \citet{Ambroise2002}. The hybrid search computes all importance scores within each fold, on training data only, but searching over many feature subsets still adds complexity even when each individual selection is unbiased \citep{Tibshirani2015}. The budget caps the number of features the downstream model sees, which limits how far it can fit noise in the rest. The selected features are also fixed inputs, fingerprint bits, descriptors or frozen embeddings, rather than fitted parameters, and we consider choosing among them less expressive than learning new features. Finally, overfit selection would give feature sets that differ widely between folds, which the Nogueira index measures (Section~\ref{sec:method:comparators}).

