Chapter~\ref{chap:flaws} showed where the similarity assumption fails on standard representations. In most of them, the distance between two molecules barely rises with the difference in their activity. Any method that trusts a molecule's neighbors inherits this problem, from predicting a molecule's activity from its nearest neighbors to finding a noisy label by comparing it with its neighbors' labels. Chapter~\ref{chap:method} introduced the adaptive hybrid representation, a set of features selected from several base representations for one task. The original idea behind it was to select the features relevant to the task first and then compute distances on those features alone, and this chapter returns to that idea.

% TODO: manual review
% DECISION 2026-10-09 (accepted without review): two research questions. The old third question, on noise
% mitigation and noise-robust training, moved to Chapter 7.
We use a hybrid for similarity in two ways. In the first, the search of Chapter~\ref{chap:method} is run with a nearest-neighbor model as the downstream model, and distances are measured in the hybrid it selects. In the second, the search ranks each candidate hybrid by how well each molecule's activity is predicted from its five nearest neighbors in it, and no downstream model is fitted. We ask two research questions:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Does distance in a hybrid track activity better than distance in the best single representation?
    \item Do the methods built on that distance work better in a hybrid? We test prediction from nearest neighbors, activity cliffs, Bayesian optimization, distance to the training molecules as a guide to prediction error, retrieval of neighbors and clustering.
\end{enumerate}

