The score uses the Manhattan distance of Chapter~\ref{chap:flaws}, on features scaled as above. Each standardized feature adds a term of similar size to this distance. So a representation's weight in the distance, which the allocation method sets, is roughly its number of features divided by the budget. A feature that only takes the values 0 and 1 adds less, so a fingerprint weighs less than this per feature. The neighbor score uses no importance weighting.

