A fold of the search is undefined when it has fewer than 10 held-out molecules or fewer than 2 groups, or when its labels or predictions are constant. A candidate hybrid with an undefined fold is refused, rather than ranked on fewer folds. We do not run the similarity search where its score is undefined:
\begin{itemize}
    \item The four Ginkgo developability datasets in this chapter (AC-SINS self-association, SMAC colloidal stability, CHO polyreactivity and expression titer) use the competition's five folds as their five groups. Inside an outer fold, each held-out block of the search is then a single group, so no molecule has a neighbor outside its own group.
    \item On binding free energy, one protein complex holds 69 of the 429 rows. On outer folds 1 to 4 it falls alone into one held-out block of the search, and every candidate is refused. We skip this dataset on all five folds.
\end{itemize}


\subsection{Nearest-Neighbor Settings}\label{sec:similarity:knn_settings}

% TODO: manual review
% Checked against similarity_helpers.knn_axes (lines 153-173), noise/similarity_space.py:228-231 and
% src/kirby/similarity/neighbour_weighting.py:214-232 on 2026-10-09.
Every experiment that predicts from neighbors or measures retrieval scores each feature space at every setting of one fixed list, instead of at one chosen setting. On datasets of up to 5,000 molecules, the distance is Manhattan, cosine or Minkowski with $p = 0.5$. Above 5,000 molecules (hERG blocking, LogD and QM9), we use only Manhattan and cosine. The number of neighbors is 5, 10 or 20. How much each neighbor counts follows one of two schemes, by rank or by inverse squared distance \citep{shepard1968two}:
\begin{equation}
w_{ij} = \frac{1}{r_{ij}} \quad \text{or} \quad w_{ij} = \frac{1}{d_{ij}^2},
\qquad
\hat{y}_i = \frac{\sum_{j \in N_k(i)} w_{ij}\, y_j}{\sum_{j \in N_k(i)} w_{ij}},
\label{eq:knn_weights}
\end{equation}
where $r_{ij}$ is the rank of training molecule $j$ among the $k$ nearest neighbors of $i$, with the nearest ranked 1. On classification data, each class receives the summed weight of its neighbors. We found no published source for the rank scheme. A molecule's own scaffold group is never excluded from its neighbors, only the molecule itself. The one experiment that does not use this list is the one on all representations joined (Section~\ref{sec:similarity:experiments}).
