\paragraph{Importance weighting.}

% TODO: manual review
% DECISION 2026-10-09 (author): describe the two weightings as they are and say where they differ; no
% re-run. Code read 2026-10-09: first formula, src/kirby/similarity/preprocessing.py:381-470 through
% compute_distance_matrix (s02, s08). Second formula, tests/experiments/_shared.py:3388-3414
% importance_feature_weights = max(s,0)^0.5 with no division, used as is by s04 (s04:370-396), s05
% (s05:376-390) and s06; s10 divides by the mean (s10:190-211, weight_convention mean1); s13 divides by the
% sum (s13:772-773, retrieval on hybrids only, clustering never weighted); c04 divides by the sum
% (c04:137-145). A constant divisor scales every distance by the same factor, so it changes neither the
% neighbors nor the rank or 1/d^2 weights. Importance source: s02 refits lightgbm_gain on the hybrid's own
% features (s02:43-55); s13 recomputes on the hybrid with its search's method (s13:765-771); s05, s06, s08,
% s10 use the search's stored importance; singles use lightgbm_gain (s02, s04, s08), lightgbm_gain else
% treeshap (s05:103-124, s10), or the distance hybrid's method (s06:419-428).
% Wettschereck1997 (METHOD_PROVENANCE.md, n12 line) added to refs.bib 2026-10-09, checked on Crossref.
Some experiments multiply each feature by a weight taken from its importance before computing the distance, $\mathbf{x}' = \mathbf{x} \odot \mathbf{w}$, so that important features count for more \citep{Wettschereck1997}. Negative importances are set to 0. For a single representation, the importance $\mathbf{s}$ is computed on the fold's training molecules, with LightGBM gain in most experiments. For a hybrid, it is the importance its search used to select the features, with two exceptions. Distance against activity refits LightGBM gain on the hybrid's features together, and retrieval recomputes the search's importance on the hybrid as a whole. We use two formulas for $\mathbf{w}$:
\begin{itemize}
    \item for distance against activity and the best number of features, $\mathbf{w} = (\mathbf{s}/\bar{s})^{a}$, with $a = 1$ under Manhattan distance, $a = 0.5$ under cosine and $a = 2$ under Minkowski with $p = 0.5$;
    \item for every other experiment that weights, $\mathbf{w} = \mathbf{s}^{1/2}$ under every distance.
\end{itemize}
Some experiments divide the second formula by a constant, which changes neither the ranking of distances nor any prediction from neighbors. The two formulas give the same neighbors only under cosine distance. Under Manhattan distance, a feature with four times the average importance counts four times as much as an average feature under the first formula, but only twice as much under the second. So the weighted distances of Sections~\ref{sec:similarity:results_similarity} and~\ref{sec:similarity:results_knn} are the same distance only under cosine. No experiment in this chapter varies the exponent.


