\paragraph{Prediction from nearest neighbors.}
% TODO: manual review
% Checked against s05_knn_prediction.py:1-140 and 425-480 and _shared.fold_reselection on 2026-10-09.
We predict each held-out molecule's activity from its nearest training molecules (Equation~\ref{eq:knn_weights}), and make two comparisons. The first was fixed before the run: the distance hybrid against the single representation, both unweighted, under Manhattan distance with 5 neighbors counted by rank. The second picks a setting again for each held-out fold, using the other four folds' held-out scores. On the hybrid side, it picks among up to six hybrids (the four predictive hybrids, the distance hybrid and the similarity hybrid), weighted and unweighted, at every distance, neighbor count and scheme. That gives up to 216 combinations. On the single side, it picks among the nearest-neighbor model's two single representations, unweighted only, which gives 36. On the three datasets with two distances, the numbers are 144 and 24, and where the similarity hybrid is undefined they are 180 and 36. So the two sides differ in two ways at once: the hybrid side picks from five or six times as many combinations, and only the hybrid side can use importance weighting.
