% Minimum of 10 applies to cliff and non-cliff molecules (s12:108-113). Models: two singles and two hybrids
% per model, headline hybrid1 vs base1 (s12:43-60, 878-916).
An activity cliff is a pair of structurally similar molecules with very different activity \citep{Stumpfe2012,vanTilborg2022}. We decide which molecules are cliff molecules in each outer fold, from its training molecules. Two training molecules are structurally similar when their ECFP4 Tanimoto similarity is at least 0.55 \citep{Hu2015}. The usual activity side of the rule, a gap of two log units \citep{Hu2015}, flags no molecule at all on Caco-2. So instead, we solve for the gap that puts 15\% of the training molecules in a similar pair whose labels differ by more than it. On classification data, a similar pair counts when its two molecules are in different classes. A held-out molecule is a cliff molecule when it has at least two similar training molecules whose labels span more than that gap. Its own label is never read. Datasets without SMILES use all representations joined instead of ECFP4, with similarity $1/(1+d)$, and the top 1\% of training pairs count as similar. Each downstream model is fitted on its two best single representations and its two best hybrids. Each fit is scored separately on the cliff molecules and on the other held-out molecules. The main comparison is the first hybrid against the best single representation. A score needs at least 10 cliff molecules and 10 other molecules in the fold.

\paragraph{Distance against prediction error.}
% TODO: manual review
% DECISION 2026-10-09 (author agreed): the interval half of this experiment is removed.
% [CLAUDE 2026-10-09, methods check] s06_applicability_domain.py:22-63, 486-541: two error vectors per model
% (all representations joined; the model's own first hybrid); distance at 5, 10 and 20 neighbors under every
% distance; mean is the default statistic (KIRBY_S06_AD_STATISTIC). Classification error = 1 - P(true class).
Each downstream model is fitted twice, on all representations joined and on its own first hybrid, and we record its error on every held-out molecule. On regression data this is the absolute error. On classification data it is one minus the predicted probability of the true class. We then compute the Spearman correlation between that error and the mean distance from the molecule to its nearest training molecules. This is done at 5, 10 and 20 neighbors and every distance, in each feature space of Section~\ref{sec:similarity:spaces}, unweighted and weighted.

\paragraph{Retrieval and clustering.}
% TODO: manual review
% Checked against s13_clustering_retrieval.py:8-30 and 251-370 on 2026-10-09.
Retrieval measures how close in activity a molecule's neighbors are. For each held-out molecule, the label gap is the weighted mean absolute difference between its label and those of its nearest training molecules, at every setting of Section~\ref{sec:similarity:knn_settings}. On classification data, it is one minus the weighted share of neighbors in the molecule's class. Lower is better. For clustering, we fit k-means on the training molecules with 3, 5 and 10 clusters, under squared Euclidean distance, and assign each held-out molecule to a cluster. Label coherence is one minus the mean standard deviation of the held-out labels within a cluster, divided by the standard deviation of all held-out labels. On classification data, it is the mean share of the largest class in each cluster. Higher is better. Clusters with fewer than two held-out molecules are left out.

