To find the best number of features, we take each downstream model's top hybrid at every budget of its search, and the distance search's top hybrid at every budget of its own. We then record the budget where prediction peaks and the budget where the correlation of distance against activity peaks. On Caco-2, LogD, Buchwald--Hartwig and SMAC colloidal stability, the two lists of budgets only share 500 features. On hERG $K_i$ and Buchwald--Hartwig only, we simulate choosing which molecules to measure. Starting from a seed set of whole scaffold groups holding at least 5\% of the molecules, a surrogate model picks 100 molecules one at a time by expected improvement. We repeat this with three seed sets, and the score is the area under the best-found-so-far curve. Last, for each representation a predictive hybrid draws from, we compare the label information in the features it kept against a random subset of the same size. Each block of features is reduced to at most 50 principal components. A mutual-information estimate (\citealp{Kraskov2004} for continuous labels, \citealp{Ross2014} for classes) is summed over the components, and the mean over three label permutations is subtracted. This sum counts information shared by two components twice.



