\section{Conclusions}\label{sec:similarity:conclusions}

% TODO: manual review
% Numbers repeated from Sections 6.3-6.6 above; sources there.
On the five small-molecule datasets, a hybrid built for distance repairs much of what Chapter~\ref{chap:flaws} found wrong with distance in standard representations. Distance tracks activity more closely on all five, and prediction from nearest neighbors improves on all five, at the setting fixed before the run and with the setting picked again. Neighbors retrieved in the hybrid have closer labels on all five. On hERG $K_i$, for example, the correlation between distance and activity difference goes from 0.0729 to 0.2625 Spearman, and prediction from five neighbors from 0.6280 to 0.5822 RMSE.

% TODO: manual review
Some things the hybrid does not fix. Weighting features by importance does part of the same work without any selection, and on LogD the weighted single representation tracks activity better than the unweighted hybrid. Distance in the hybrid tracks prediction error worse on hERG blocking. The kept features carry less label information than principal components of the same size on 11 of 13 datasets. Activity cliffs on QM9 could not be judged, because only 48 of its held-out molecules are cliff molecules.

% TODO: manual review
% Links to the central claim, outline 8.2 and 8.2a.
On the antibody datasets and Buchwald--Hartwig the answer depends on the dataset. Distance tracks activity more closely on all of them. With the setting picked again, prediction from nearest neighbors is worse in the hybrid on four of the seven antibody datasets and on Buchwald--Hartwig, and retrieval is worse on binding free energy and Buchwald--Hartwig. These losses fit the central claim of this thesis: the right input depends on the data, and a hybrid is an input chosen for one dataset, not a fix that holds everywhere.

