\paragraph{Correcting clipped labels.}\label{sec:similarity:censoring_lift}
% TODO: review, needs manual review
% [2026-09-30] Formula checked against the code: /Volumes/seagate/local_noise_run/loop_headroom/censoring_probe.py (tobit = limit + sd * mills; lift = tobit - limit) and constant_lift in tests/experiments/noise/n30_censored_likelihood.py at 4a7f31e. The truncated-normal mean above the limit is a different quantity (n30's truncated_normal_mean arm, 0.6367 pKi for random forest on ECFP4, REPORT.md line 1684); it is not used in this chapter.
Labels clipped at a detection limit are corrected by raising every training label at the limit by one shared amount. The limit $L$ is read from the clipped training labels as their largest value, and the molecules at the limit are those whose label equals it. A model fitted on the clipped training labels gives an out-of-fold prediction $p_i$ for each training molecule, and $\sigma$ is the standard deviation of $y_i - p_i$ over the molecules below the limit. Each molecule at the limit contributes
\begin{equation}
\ell_i = \sigma\,\frac{\phi(a_i)}{1 - \Phi(a_i)}, \qquad a_i = \frac{L - p_i}{\sigma},
\end{equation}
where $\phi$ and $\Phi$ are the standard normal density and distribution function. The shared lift is the mean of $\ell_i$ over the molecules at the limit. It is not the distance of the truncated normal mean above the limit, which is $p_i - L + \ell_i$. Each result states which model gave the predictions $p_i$.

\subsubsection{Sample Removal Methods}
% [notes moved to thesis_notes_moved.md]

Distance-based approaches rely on the assumption that similar molecules have similar properties \citep{Cherkasov2014}. Presumably, noisy data would break this pattern, making it a method for detecting anomalous data. $k$-nearest-neighbor filtering examines the variance of a sample's neighborhood. For sample $x_i$ with $k$ nearest neighbors $\mathcal{N}_k(x_i)$, we compute the standard deviation of neighbor labels:
\begin{equation}
\sigma_i = \text{std}(\{y_j : x_j \in \mathcal{N}_k(x_i)\})
\end{equation}
Samples with $\sigma_i$ exceeding the 90th percentile are marked as potentially noisy. We test $k \in \{5, 10, 20\}$.

Local Outlier Factor (LOF) compares the local density around a sample with the local densities around its $k$ nearest neighbors \citep{Breunig2000}; a sample in a sparser region than its neighbors receives a high LOF. We flag the 10\% of training molecules with the highest LOF, using 20 neighbors. % TODO: REVIEW MODIFICATION -- formula checked against the code on 2026-09-29: the code flags the top 10% (contamination=0.1, n_neighbors=20), not LOF > 2 (src/kirby/utils/noise_mitigation_methods.py:547).

% [CLAUDE 2026-10-09] citation fix: these are the random-forest filters in noise_mitigation_methods.py (CoTeachingFilter :1030, DivideMixFilter :1088), adapted from the papers, not the deep-network methods themselves. Check these results came from those filters and not from lnl_methods.py.
We also tested the following noise removal methods: distance-weighted prediction filtering, neighborhood consensus filtering, activity-cliff detection, Mahalanobis outlier detection, cross-validation disagreement, bootstrap ensemble filtering, a filter adapted from co-teaching \citep{Han2018}, in which two random forests each train on the other's lowest-residual 70\%, and a filter adapted from DivideMix \citep{li2020dividemix}, which fits a two-component Gaussian mixture to out-of-bag residuals.

