Four further methods are tested on the recorded labels with nothing injected. C-Mixup \citep{yao2022cmixup}, a form of mixup \citep{Zhang2018mixup}, adds one training molecule for each existing one by mixing its z-scored features and label with those of a partner that has a similar label. The partner $j$ of molecule $i$ is drawn with probability proportional to $\exp(-(y_i-y_j)^2/2h^2)$, with $h$ equal to 0.2 standard deviations of the training labels, and the mixing weight is drawn from Beta(2, 2). Target-noise bagging averages the predictions of 5 models, each fitted on the labels plus fresh Gaussian noise. The noise standard deviation is 0.5 times that of the residuals of out-of-fold LightGBM predictions, made on 5 scaffold-group folds of the training molecules in the same representation. Pair down-weighting takes the closest 1\% of training pairs and gives weight 0.25 to both molecules of any pair whose labels differ by more than $3\sqrt{2}\,\hat\sigma$, where $\hat\sigma$ is the noise standard deviation estimated from the label differences of close pairs. Down-weighting suspect molecules lowers the weight of training molecules with a high noise score (Table~\ref{tab:noise_estimators}), over 11 settings of the score and the strength.

