The survey of twelve methods comes from an earlier preliminary run on nanobody thermostability (672 nanobodies), scored by the Spearman correlation between predicted and measured melting temperature on held-out nanobodies (higher is better), as the mean of 3 folds. The folds are grouped by 95\% sequence identity cluster (309 clusters). Gaussian noise with a standard deviation of 0.5 or 1.5 times that of all 672 measured melting temperatures (10.12\,$^\circ$C) was added to the training labels, with one noise draw per level, and the held-out labels carry no added noise. Table~\ref{tab:ch6prelim_lnl} gives the score of each of the twelve methods, whose settings are listed in Additional file~\ref{app:add:lnl_settings}, beside a plain neural network trained without any noise handling. The plain network is the fully connected network of that Additional file, and every method except the heteroscedastic GP trains this same network. The heteroscedastic GP predicts with a Gaussian process (RBF kernel, at most 1,000 training nanobodies), and a small network estimates each nanobody's noise variance. The twelve methods include the four removal methods of the exploratory study in Section~\ref{sec:lnl_motivation}. One of them, small-loss selection, trains a new network only on the 70\% of training nanobodies with the smallest error after a warm-up period. In these runs, removal by distance to neighbors and MentorNet found neighbors by Euclidean distance on the standardized features.

