The inputs were one-hot (all 5,600 features), the hybrid, and the weighted hybrid, each standardized on the training fold. In each fold the hybrid was built from that fold's noisy training labels before the network was trained on it. Its budget of 50 features, linear allocation and LightGBM gain importance were fixed. The only setting searched was which representations it draws on: the three with the highest standalone scores in the fold, or all ten of Table~\ref{tab:dataset_reps}. A random forest on 3 inner folds of the training nanobodies made this choice in each fold and at each noise level. At 1.5 times the label standard deviation, folds 1 and 2 used the three and fold 3 used all ten; which representations were used at 0.5 times could not be recovered. The 50 features were divided among the chosen representations in proportion to each representation's Spearman score with the same random forest, which served as the internal evaluator. The weighted hybrid multiplies the standardized hybrid features by the square root of each feature's LightGBM gain importance in that fold, with the multipliers rescaled to a mean of 1, and is not rescaled afterwards.

