In the preliminary runs on hERG $K_i$, predicting each molecule's p$K_i$ from its five nearest neighbors gave a lower RMSE in the similarity hybrid than in any of the five single representations. Noise detection and estimation await the full runs. Under censoring at 10\% of the hERG $K_i$ training labels, raising every clipped label by one shared amount gave a lower mean RMSE than the clipped labels for every model and feature space tested, whereas deleting the clipped molecules gave the highest. Under Gaussian noise on nanobody thermostability, every one of twelve methods scored higher on the hybrid than any of them did on one-hot. With nothing injected, whether a method helped depended on the model, the representation and, on humanness, the representation in which the noise score was computed. Which method helps therefore depends on the type of noise, the dataset and the feature space, and Chapter~\ref{chap:discussion} returns to this.


